<QuestionLayout
  title="How do I publish a capability to the Traverse registry?"
  description="Primary: plain-English skill → human-reviewed registry PR. Secondary: manual Rust with coverage gates and digest-stable publish metadata. No side-door upload."
  canonical="https://traverse-framework.com/questions/how-do-i-publish-a-capability-to-the-registry.html"
  category="Contracts and Capabilities"
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p>The Traverse registry is a git repository with CI, not a free-form CDN. A capability becomes public when a pull request merges with a valid contract, a digest-addressed WASM artifact, and the publish metadata the pipeline expects.</p>

  <h2>Primary path — plain English via the Claude skill</h2>
  <p>For most authors, start with <code>traverse-capability-author</code> in <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/claude-skills/tree/main/skills/traverse-capability-author" target="_blank" rel="noopener">traverse-framework/claude-skills</a>. The skill interviews you, checks the registry, writes the contract, produces executable WASM, and opens a human-reviewed PR. You describe the business rule; you review the PR; you do not have to write the Rust yourself. Under the hood the implementation is still Rust→WASM. Details: <a href="/questions/how-do-i-author-a-capability-in-plain-english.html">How do I author a capability in plain English?</a></p>

  <h2>Secondary path — manual Rust in the registry repo</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Work in <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/registry" target="_blank" rel="noopener">traverse-framework/registry</a>. Read <code>CONTRIBUTING.md</code> before scaffolding.</li>
    <li>Add the capability source (Rust tests included) and the contract that describes inputs, outputs, and placement expectations.</li>
    <li>Use the documented <code>traverse-cli</code> capability publish / scaffold path so <code>artifact.digest</code> and <code>artifact.url</code> are produced correctly — hand-edited digests usually fail CI.</li>
    <li>Meet the coverage gates the registry enforces (function coverage at 100%, line/region thresholds in the CONTRIBUTING notes).</li>
    <li>Run the repo's pre-PR check script, open a PR with <code>## Governing Spec</code> and <code>## Validation</code> filled, and wait for CI.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What "published" means</h2>
  <p>After merge, the capability is part of the catalog the next index release ships to <a href="https://registry.traverse-framework.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">registry.traverse-framework.com</a>. Callers discover it by reference (name@version) and verify the artifact digest. There is no maintainer-only upload that skips the PR. Contributions are fork-and-PR; collaborator seats are not the first-publish path.</p>

  <h2>A good first publish</h2>
  <p>If you want a tiny first capability rather than inventing a domain model, see the labeled starter <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/registry/issues/485" target="_blank" rel="noopener">registry#485 (text.slugify)</a>. For business-shaped rules agents should not free-hand, see registry issues <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/registry/issues/493" target="_blank" rel="noopener">#493</a>–<a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/registry/issues/507" target="_blank" rel="noopener">#507</a> and the <a href="/blog/why-next-help-wanted-is-business-shaped.html">help-wanted write-up</a>.</p>
</QuestionLayout>
